-- title:
--   History-adapted on-line hypothesis sequence h_t
-- statement:
--   **p. 202, PDF p. 219.** The hypothesis $h_t$ generated by an on-line algorithm $A$ after
--   sequentially processing the first $t$ pairs of a labeled sample $S$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $A$ is modeled as a function of the *prefix* of the sample seen so
--   far, `A : (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → X × ℝ) → (X → ℝ)`; `OnlineHypothesis A S t` applies `A` to
--   `S`'s first `t` pairs only, guaranteeing `h_t` never depends on the pair drawn at round `t`
--   itself — the history-adaptedness Lemma 8.14's martingale argument needs.
-- source:
--   Mohri, Rostamizadeh & Talwalkar, Foundations of Machine Learning, 2nd ed., MIT Press 2018, p. 202 (PDF p. 219)

import Mathlib

namespace FoundationsML.OnlineLearning

/-- The hypothesis `h_t` generated by an on-line algorithm `A` after sequentially processing
the first `t` pairs of a labeled sample `S : Fin T → X × ℝ` (Mohri, Rostamizadeh & Talwalkar,
*Foundations of Machine Learning*, 2nd ed., MIT Press 2018, p. 202, PDF p. 219, "the sequence
of hypotheses generated by an on-line algorithm `A` sequentially processing `S`").

**Formalization Note.** `A : (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → X × ℝ) → (X → ℝ)` models the on-line
algorithm as a function of the *prefix* of the sample seen so far; `OnlineHypothesis A S t`
is `A` applied to the first `t.val` pairs of `S` (i.e., `S_0,…,S_{t-1}`, not `S_t` itself).
This type signature is what makes `h_t` history-adapted (measurable with respect to the
sample seen strictly before round `t`), the property Lemma 8.14's martingale argument needs
(p. 202, PDF p. 219) — `h_t` cannot depend on the pair `S_t` drawn at round `t` itself. -/
def OnlineHypothesis {X : Type*} {T : ℕ}
    (A : (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → X × ℝ) → (X → ℝ)) (S : Fin T → X × ℝ) (t : Fin T) : X → ℝ :=
  A t.val (fun i => S (Fin.castLE (Nat.le_of_lt t.isLt) i))

end FoundationsML.OnlineLearning


